ion  there's six uh military transport planes  that are flying between kabul and  tashkent  that they have now evacuated more than 2  700 people 1 800 of them are said to be  afghani and the others are members of 38  different nations so  there is an operation going on this  morning another plane has been going to  kabul um and once they arrive in germany  the people are being distributed across  the country  dw political correspondent hans plan  thanks so much  now as the taliban consolidate control  in afghanistan two decades after they  were removed from power the group's  leadership appears to be taking shape  some have been part of the taliban since  its creation in the 1990s so let's take  a look at some of the key figures  has been the taliban supreme leader  since 2016. prior to his rise to the top  he spent decades as a strict hard-line  judge and cleric he has final authority  on military political and religious  affairs  abdul ghani bharadha is a co-founder of  the taliban and is now their political  head he held senior positions in the  late 1990s baradar spent eight years in  prison in pakistan after being captured  in 2010. he helped lead negotiations  with the us to withdraw troops from  afghanistan  last year  the deputy leader of the taliban is  believed to be surajudan haqqani now he  is head of the haqqani network in  pakistan and afghanistan and he led many  of their military efforts the haqqani  network has been a fierce opponent of  the u.s president's presence in  afghanistan and haqqani is on the fbi's  most wanted list and is believed to be  behind several terror attacks now the  head of the taliban's military  operations is muhammad yaqub there are  no known pictures of him he's the son of  the deceased taliban founder muller  muhammad omar  now for more on this i'm joined now in  the studio by dw sandra peterson peters  sandra is from dw's research and  investigations team  and has been covering afghanistan for  decades sandra thanks so mu